    "interpretation": "The monitored-boundary electron loss rate changes by a factor of about three from 16 to 32 particles/cell while the ion rate increases by about 40%. The net left-boundary charge rate moves toward zero, from -1.61 A to -0.21 A, and the reverse-event fraction rises from 4.8% to 16.5%. Higher reverse-event frequency is therefore consistent with population-dependent convergence of electron and ion loss rates, not controller instability. Within the fine run, ion current is stable between half-windows while electron current is bursty (coefficient of variation 1.76 and second/first-half magnitude ratio 0.41), so this 5 ns average is statistically inadequate.",
    "next_gate": "Separate transient evolution from sampling noise with multiple adjacent windows or independent seeds at the 32-particle population before changing the cathode acceptance rule."
